    <div class="fact-grid">
      <div class="fact-card">
        <div class="icon">🏛️</div>
        <h3>Ancient Origins</h3>
        <p>Chopsticks originated in ancient China around 5,000 years ago, initially used for cooking rather than eating. They became common eating utensils during the Han Dynasty (206 BCE – 220 CE).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fact-card">
        <div class="icon">🌳</div>
        <h3>Environmental Impact</h3>
        <p>China alone produces approximately 80 billion disposable chopsticks annually, using around 20 million trees. This has led to growing movements for reusable alternatives.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fact-card">
        <div class="icon">🧠</div>
        <h3>Brain Benefits</h3>
        <p>Using chopsticks engages over 30 joints and 50 muscles, stimulating brain activity and improving fine motor skills. Studies suggest regular use may enhance cognitive function.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fact-card">
        <div class="icon">📐</div>
        <h3>Perfect Length</h3>
        <p>Traditional chopsticks measure 7 Chinese inches (about 23cm) — considered the lucky ratio of 7:6 representing Earth and sky, connecting humans to the universe.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fact-card">
        <div class="icon">🔬</div>
        <h3>Material Science</h3>
        <p>Chopsticks are crafted from bamboo, wood, metal, plastic, jade, ivory, and even precious metals. Each material offers unique benefits for grip, heat resistance, and durability.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fact-card">
        <div class="icon">⚡</div>
        <h3>Speed Eating</h3>
        <p>The Guinness World Record for most M&Ms eaten with chopsticks in one minute is 65, demonstrating the remarkable precision these simple tools can achieve.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

